// NEGATIVE CONTROL for `// EXPECT_FAIL:`. The runner must report FAIL for this
// file, and the self-test block inverts the verdict accordingly.
//
// This program COMPILES. It carries `// EXPECT_FAIL:` anyway. So if the runner
// reports anything other than FAIL, EXPECT_FAIL is not being enforced — and
// every ported rejection test in tests/regression/*_rejected.wyn would then be
// passing vacuously, because their only assertion is that compilation fails.
//
// Why a control is needed at all rather than trusting the implementation: a
// rejection directive is satisfied by the ABSENCE of success, and this repo has
// been burned repeatedly by assertions that pass when the thing they watch
// disappears. The directive cannot prove itself; only a program it must reject
// can.
//
// Keep this program trivially valid. If it ever stops compiling for an unrelated
// reason the control still reports FAIL and the self-test still passes — i.e. it
// would silently stop testing anything. That is why it is one statement with no
// dependencies, no imports and no annotations.
// EXPECT_FAIL:
println("this program compiles, so EXPECT_FAIL must reject it")
